[["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "-7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "q-138067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "q-138074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "q-138074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,2,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "q-138074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4c0e7e08f72cdae407343963a3ab5ff66cf8ffcdc5c30fe4db1f955ab6c1248d"} {"question" "q-138074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "q-138074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "q-138074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/state.json" "file_sha256" "c68fa87c4ac68aad1563f499ac85dfd254842abcef2884ede275189eab9b9e07"} {"question" "e-12659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 - 5x_2^2 +3x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/example-12659.json" "file_sha256" "8d5811b36f734f64b6be4cec1ca7f1b5120d221191dbebb8a1519592c1b4a63f"} {"question" "e-12659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0$ and $x_2^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have that \n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 3x_1^2, \\qquad -5x_2^2 \\leq 3x_2^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 3.$\n\nNotice that when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/assets/1986d7b40e318db91ac16ba9540ec704fd6221ac295dec66540c190a090a1463.png) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2}+3(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/example-12659.json" "file_sha256" "8d5811b36f734f64b6be4cec1ca7f1b5120d221191dbebb8a1519592c1b4a63f"} {"question" "e-12660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=5x_1^2 - 4x_2^2 - 6x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/example-12660.json" "file_sha256" "728e25e3ab789bec081e965cd09542930df45d8c9835f11bf2b03965aa0ec1c1"} {"question" "e-12660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0$ and $x_2^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5x_1^2 \\geq -6x_1^2, \\qquad -4x_2^2 \\geq -6x_2^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-6(1)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq -6.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no smaller than $-6.$ But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = -6.$\n\nNotice that when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/assets/d509eb79bc8d9a96b60922166e49d0225eac7a99d2614859c31af15199d4d47c.png) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}-4(0)^{2}-6(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $-6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/example-12660.json" "file_sha256" "728e25e3ab789bec081e965cd09542930df45d8c9835f11bf2b03965aa0ec1c1"} {"question" "e-12661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2-2x_3^2+2x_2x_3$  given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/example-12661.json" "file_sha256" "e881c28930c0295edd04de597ab9c8d6aa4ec3000b933a1752bc02ed80ed6c82"} {"question" "e-12661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/assets/d7716e2c2924212e880275dd674d4a951daf66ac41e5a888a57acb6be686118f.png)\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070559/example-12661.json" "file_sha256" "e881c28930c0295edd04de597ab9c8d6aa4ec3000b933a1752bc02ed80ed6c82"}] "usage" {"e-12661" [] "e-12659" [] "q-138019" [] "e-12660" []} "basis" 2491 "retractions" [[17592186167039 :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x) = 5x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖ = 1$."] [17592186166423 :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 - 5x_2^2 +3x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$."] [17592186166423 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\nx_1^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_2^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 3x_1^2, \\qquad -5x_2^2 \\leq 3x_2^2\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 2x_{1}^{2} - 5x_{2}^{2} + 3x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&\\le 3x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{2}^{2} + 3x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\nx_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n3(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}) = 3(1) = 3\n$$\n\nand, in turn,\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 3\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $3$. But before we conclude that $3$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = 3\n$$\n\nNotice that when $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 2x_{1}^{2} - 5x_{2}^{2} + 3x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2(0)^{2} - 5(0)^{2} + 3(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nthe maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $3$."] [17592186166424 :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=5x_1^2 - 4x_2^2 - 6x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$."] [17592186166424 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\nx_1^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_2^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n5x_1^2 \\geq -6x_1^2, \\qquad -4x_2^2 \\geq -6x_2^2\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 5x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&\\ge - 6x_{1}^{2} - 6x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&=-6(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\nx_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n-6(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}) =-6(1) =-6\n$$\n\nand, in turn,\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq -6\n$$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no smaller than $-6$. But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = -6\n$$\n\nNotice that when $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= 5x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2} - 6x_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 5(0)^{2} - 4(0)^{2} - 6(1)^{2} \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nthe minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $-6$."] [17592186166425 :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2-2x_3^2+2x_2x_3$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$."] [17592186166425 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that:\n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\max \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$, is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n$\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 - λ & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)((-2 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(λ^{2} + 4λ + 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(λ + 3)(λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 1,-1,-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: \\mid \\: \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163653 17592186163654 17592186163655 17592186163656 17592186163657] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163652 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163652 :answer-field/id] [17592186163652 :answer-field/key] [17592186163652 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
